WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.080
Ever since The Walking Dead first aired in 2010,&nbsp;
the creative minds at AMC had been quietly sowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.280
seeds that connected the zombie series with&nbsp;
another AMC success story, Breaking Bad. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.240
what does Walter White's meth-infused power trip&nbsp;
have to do with surviving the end of the world?

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.800
Ever since the spinoff series of Fear&nbsp;
the Walking Dead arrived on the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.880
it's been clear that The Walking Dead universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.800
was more than just a single show set&nbsp;
between Atlanta and Washington D.C.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.080
This was confirmed when AMC Network CEO Josh&nbsp;
Sapan announced in early September 2018 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.680
the folks at AMC had plans for the universe that&nbsp;
extended, quote, "over the next decade plus."

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.920
While this may have seemed odd in the shadow&nbsp;
of the show's faltering ratings at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.600
it kind of makes sense.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.800
The idea of a long term goal that spans beyond&nbsp;
the scope of a single film or series is par for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.120
the course in Hollywood. Cross-pollinating&nbsp;
universes is totally in these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.640
so a connection between AMC&nbsp;
shows is hardly surprising.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.120
What's a bit more interesting, though, is&nbsp;
the not-quite-confirmed larger crossover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.320
that integrates Breaking Bad&nbsp;
into The Walking Dead universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.760
a phenomenon fondly nicknamed&nbsp;
the "Breaking Dead Theory."

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.800
It's been loosely confirmed&nbsp;
by showrunner Dave Erickson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.280
and here's the skinny on&nbsp;
how the two shows line up.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.240
It didn't take long for The Walking Dead&nbsp;
to start incorporating elements of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.480
runaway hit Breaking Bad into their&nbsp;
fledgling zombie apocalypse series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.280
In the second episode of season two, Daryl&nbsp;
Dixon can be seen rifling through a clear bag.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.520
The contents comprise his brother Merle's&nbsp;
stash, most of which is in pill bottles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.240
However, tucked at the bottom of the bag&nbsp;
is a hefty dose of some blue crystals.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.400
The shot immediately grabs your&nbsp;
attention, as Daryl casually lists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.040
"crystal" among the gathered narcotics. Of&nbsp;
course, the prize for the most famous blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.240
crystals in history has to go to Mr. White's&nbsp;
Sky Blue. By the end of Breaking Bad, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:55.600
stuff was sought after all over the world, so its&nbsp;
presence in Georgia wouldn't be particularly odd.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.480
The connection hung there for a bit until none&nbsp;
other than Walking Dead creator Robert Kirkman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.360
confirmed that the Easter&nbsp;
egg was there on purpose.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.640
"It ain't cloudy or dirty or nothin.&nbsp;
Just the right shade of blue."

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.000
Another possible link that was picked up&nbsp;
on in the Walking Dead season 4 episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.760
"Still" involved Walter White's&nbsp;
associate, Mr. Jesse Pinkman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.640
This one came during that chunk of time when Daryl&nbsp;
and Beth were romping around the countryside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.200
surviving, being moody, and bonding&nbsp;
like a couple of cute misfits.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.600
In one scene Daryl tells a story about his&nbsp;
brother Merle before everything went south.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:37.338
"Merle had this dealer. This janky&nbsp;
little white guy. A tweaker."

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.000
Fans were quick to jump on the description&nbsp;
as fitting Pinkman rather neatly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.520
The speculation became more intriguing&nbsp;
a little further into the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.173
when Daryl explained an altercation&nbsp;
with the fellow in question.

00:02:49.173 --> 00:02:56.480
"Then he pulls a gun, sticks it right here.&nbsp;
He says, 'I'm gonna kill you, bitch.'"

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.120
Between the description and that line, it's&nbsp;
hard not to start drawing a connection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.960
Did Merle work for the nefarious&nbsp;
Jesse Pinkman once upon a time?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.320
Perhaps. Although, at another point in&nbsp;
the story Daryl refers to the guy's kids.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.260
Guess we'll never know.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.160
Another Easter egg appeared during&nbsp;
season 3 of Fear the Walking Dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.280
hidden in plain sight. As the&nbsp;
group entered a bustling bazaar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.040
a song could be heard blaring&nbsp;
over the radio. It was in Spanish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.400
and to an uninformed viewer did little more than&nbsp;
add a mariachi-style splash of color to the scene.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.120
However, for those intimately acquainted with&nbsp;
Walter White's tragic story arc, the song had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.440
a much deeper meaning and an undeniable&nbsp;
connection to the Breaking Bad universe.&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.120
We're talking about "Negro y&nbsp;
Azul: The Ballad of Heisenberg."

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.520
The song appeared as a cold open in Breaking&nbsp;
Bad and is written about Walter White,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.760
a.k.a. Heisenberg, chronicling his&nbsp;
rise to power against established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.960
drug cartels and predicting his eventual demise.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.840
The song is clearly specific to Breaking Bad,&nbsp;
and its appearance on Fear the Walking Dead was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.520
a clear shoutout to the connection that&nbsp;
the shows share. Showrunner Erickson was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.000
referring to this scene in particular when&nbsp;
he confirmed that the nod was intentional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.080
adding more cargo to the runaway train&nbsp;
that is the Breaking Dead Theory.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:17.120
In season 4, as he continues to break bad, Walter&nbsp;
White has a growing infatuation with indulging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.560
his reckless nature. Instead of returning a car&nbsp;
for an exorbitant restocking fee, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:30.400
White simply blows it up. And what about that&nbsp;
car? A 2009 Dodge Challenger. Red with a double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.520
black racing stripe straight down the center.&nbsp;
That is, until it was blown to smithereens.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.440
And it wasn't the last time one of&nbsp;
these cars would appear on an AMC show.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.240
Fast forward to exactly one apocalypse later.&nbsp;
In the first season of The Walking Dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.640
another Challenger makes an appearance, this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:52.080
roaming the streets of Atlanta. The car&nbsp;
in question is, once again, a 2009 model.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:58.080
Some have taken a gigantic leap, assuming that the&nbsp;
mysterious Glenn who sold Walter the car in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:04.240
Mexico was none other than Glenn Rhee, who fixed&nbsp;
it up and drove it to Georgia. A more reasonable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.200
explanation might be that Glenn Rhee the car&nbsp;
salesman was simply infatuated with that model.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:13.360
Either way, this nod from the new show&nbsp;
to the old seems clearly intended.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.760
While the little Breaking Bad Easter&nbsp;
eggs sprinkled throughout The Walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.960
Dead universe are fun to point out, the real&nbsp;
heart and soul of the Breaking Dead Theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:28.080
actually rests on a crossover that's even&nbsp;
more sinister. It suggests that Blue Sky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.960
doesn't merely link the two worlds, but that&nbsp;
it's the cause of the zombie apocalypse.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.840
While the theory hasn't been made official in&nbsp;
any formal way, it also hasn't been denied by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.200
creators of either show. On the contrary, they've&nbsp;
stoked the fires. Walking Dead creator Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.760
Kirkman said, albeit a bit tongue-in-cheek,&nbsp;
quote, "That's canon, it's confirmed!"

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.160
Producer Gale Ann Hurd, when responding&nbsp;
to the question of what caused the virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.480
stated flatly, quote, "The meth&nbsp;
from Breaking Bad, for sure."

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.320
On the other side of the aisle, Breaking Bad's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.200
Vince Gilligan responded to&nbsp;
the speculation by saying:

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.800
"I love that theory. That was a&nbsp;
kick. They're two great shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.880
The Walking Dead and Fear the Walking Dead."

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:08.720
Bryan Cranston added,

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.760
"Walt is dead, so he could be a&nbsp;
zombie right now. Heisenberg zombie!"

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.960
Gale was a pretty sweet character on Breaking&nbsp;
Bad. He was one of the few people who could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.920
rival White's scientific prowess, and he could&nbsp;
brew some darn good coffee. In fact, the man had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.080
turned the latter into a science project with an&nbsp;
elaborate machine that took volume, temperature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:32.720
acidity, and tannins, all into account in&nbsp;
the pursuit of brewing the perfect cup.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:36.560
"Oh my God. That is the best&nbsp;
coffee I've ever tasted."

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.440
While his death may have left the Breaking&nbsp;
Bad crew with cups of Folgers sludge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:46.160
it appears it wasn't the last time the scientist's&nbsp;
magically caffeine-infused machine was used.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.080
In the third episode of season 3 on&nbsp;
The Walking Dead, Merle, the Governor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.120
and Milton all gather in his&nbsp;
lab. While they're talking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.640
in the background is none other&nbsp;
than Gale's coffee machine.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.720
The question is: How did it get there?

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.200
This tie-in between Breaking Bad&nbsp;
and The Walking Dead goes back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.040
further than you might think. Season 9&nbsp;
of The Walking Dead didn't just see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:12.240
departure of characters like Rick Grimes. It&nbsp;
also saw the introduction of the Whisperers.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.400
In episode ten, we saw a great deal of their&nbsp;
leader Alpha's backstory through the testimony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:22.240
of her daughter Lydia. During those scenes,&nbsp;
both of Lydia's parents are seen singing.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:29.520
"Lydia, oh! Lydia, say have you met&nbsp;
Lydia, oh! Lydia the tattooed lady. [...]"

00:07:29.783 --> 00:07:32.960
The tune is fitting for the scene as the&nbsp;
girl looks for solace amidst the horrors of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:36.960
the end of the world. But it wasn't just&nbsp;
a well-placed musical number — it also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.358
of course, harkened back to Breaking Bad.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.040
In the waning moments of&nbsp;
Breaking Bad's last episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:47.360
Walter White stands in the midst of the carnage&nbsp;
he's created in his final act of vengeance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.680
As he parts ways with Jesse Pinkman for the&nbsp;
last time, a cellphone starts to ring on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:57.840
lifeless body of Todd that lies in front of him.&nbsp;
The tune on the cell? "Lydia the Tattooed Lady."

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:04.000
"Lydia, oh! Lydia, say have you met&nbsp;
Lydia, oh! Lydia the tattooed lady."

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.080
It added an abrupt splash of color to&nbsp;
the series' swan song as White picked up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.400
and informed yet another Lydia on the other&nbsp;
end of the call that her death was imminent.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.400
The connections between AMC's two&nbsp;
monumental worlds are too numerous to deny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:22.320
and yet the creators of both series&nbsp;
have yet to openly steer into the skid.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.160
Walter White's world has continued on in the&nbsp;
form of Better Call Saul and the full-length&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:31.440
feature El Camino released in 2019. In addition,&nbsp;
The Walking Dead has Fear the Walking Dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.240
a triple-header of films in the works, and&nbsp;
several more confirmed Walking Dead series!

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.720
With all the material being produced, a&nbsp;
marriage of the two universes could be a match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:44.880
made in heaven. After all, Breaking Bad&nbsp;
hardly closed up shop before it became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.640
one of the cult classics of our time. And&nbsp;
the fandom still surrounding The Walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:53.302
Dead could continue breathing life into this&nbsp;
beloved world of the dead for years to come.

